  "findings": {
    "the_demo_task_is_trivial": "rle scored 192/192 (p=1.000), and stayed 192/192 after adding two double-digit run-length cases. The task in our own demo video is not hard for this model. Measured, not suspected.",
    "amplification_needs_p_above_zero": "gron with JSON-literal semantics scored 0/192. P = 1-(1-p)^K is 0 for every K when p is 0. Verified search multiplies capability; it cannot manufacture it. This is the boundary condition of the whole architecture, and it is measured here rather than asserted.",
    "competence_is_near_bimodal": "Across five difficulties we could not find a task in the middle. rle p=1.000; gron p in {0.0000, 0.0052, 0.0000}. Removing the array case did not help, so the barrier is container-declaration semantics, not indexing. For this model on this family, competence is close to binary.",
    "curve_fit": "At p=0.0052 the bootstrap curve tracked 1-(1-p)^K to a maximum absolute error of 0.0021 across K=1..32. CAVEAT: with a single success in the pool this is close to tautological -- resampling one positive nearly reproduces the binomial by construction. It is consistent with the law, not strong evidence for it.",
    "policy_consequence": "Value of verified search is zero at p=0, zero at p=1, and maximal in between -- but the K-sweep shows the COST is near-zero up to K=32 (1.21x wall clock for 25.8x the tokens). So the correct policy is to run large K always: it is nearly free, and it pays exactly when p is in the middle."
  },
